Given that $S$ is the midpoint of $PQ$, we have $SQ = PQ/2 = 40/2 = 20$.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\coordinate [label=above:$P$] (P) at (0,2);
\coordinate [label=left:$Q$] (Q) at (-1.5,0);
\coordinate [label=right:$R$] (R) at (1.5,0);
\coordinate [label=below:$S$] (S) at (0,0);
\coordinate [label=right:$T$] (T) at (0,1.2);

\draw (P) -- (Q) -- (R) -- cycle;
\draw (S) -- (T);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Now, triangle $TRS$ is a $45^\circ$-$45^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle, since $R$ is a right angle and $TS$ is perpendicular to $PS$. Therefore, $ST = SQ/\sqrt{2} = 20/\sqrt{2} = 10\sqrt{2}$.

Thus, the length of $ST$ is $\boxed{10 \sqrt{2}}$.

Our triangle setup is:

[asy]
size(100);
draw((0,0)--(12,0)--(0,9)--cycle,black+linewidth(1));
draw(rightanglemark((12,0),(0,0),(0,9),20),black+linewidth(1));
label("$R$",(0,0),W);
label("$P$",(12,0),E);
label("$Q$",(0,9),W);
label("12",(0,0)--(12,0),S);
[/asy]

Since $\tan{P}=\frac{3}{4}$, we have
$$ \frac{QR}{PR} = \frac{3}{4}, $$
so
$$ QR = \frac{3}{4} \times 12 = 9. $$

Now, applying the Pythagorean theorem:
$$ PQ = \sqrt{QR^2 + PR^2} = \sqrt{9^2 + 12^2} = \sqrt{81 + 144} = \sqrt{225} = \boxed{15}. $$

